      "jurisdiction": "Wisconsin state courts",
      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-21",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "State v. Polashek",
          "citation": "2002 WI 74, 253 Wis. 2d 527, 646 N.W.2d 330",
          "court": "Wisconsin Supreme Court",
          "year": 2002,
          "date_decided": "2002-06-26",
          "holding": "The strict-liability prohibition in Wis. Stat. sec. 48.981(7) on disclosing child-abuse report information reaches only communications of information the recipient did not already know.",
          "relevance": "It is the criminal backstop that limits what child-abuse report information can be released to the public.",
          "quote": "we hold, for the purposes of § 48.981(7), that \"disclose\" is defined to require that the information communicated must have been previously unknown by the recipient",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2013929/state-v-polashek/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2013929
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Linzmeyer v. Forcey",
          "citation": "2002 WI 84, 254 Wis. 2d 306, 646 N.W.2d 811",
          "court": "Wisconsin Supreme Court",
          "year": 2002,
          "date_decided": "2002-07-02",
          "holding": "Wisconsin's public records law applies to a completed police investigative report and creates a strong presumption in favor of release; here the subject's privacy and reputational interests did not overcome that presumption.",
          "relevance": "It is the controlling Wisconsin open-records balancing test for transparency requests.",
          "quote": "We hold that the open records law applies to the Report in this case and there is thus a strong presumption in favor of release.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2013841/linzmeyer-v-forcey/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2013841
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re Peter B.",
          "citation": "184 Wis. 2d 57, 516 N.W.2d 746 (Ct. App. 1994)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Wisconsin",
          "year": 1994,
          "date_decided": "1994-04-05",
          "holding": "A juvenile court may order disclosure of otherwise confidential agency records under Wis. Stat. sec. 48.78(2)(a) as a reasonable exercise of its discretion, and that order was lawful here.",
          "relevance": "It is the confidentiality exception that makes transparency in Wisconsin juvenile records possible.",
          "quote": "the juvenile court order for disclosure was a reasonable exercise of lawful authority under § 48.78(2)(a), STATS.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1865112/in-interest-of-peter-b/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1865112
        }
      ],
